bir_flow_summary_seed_underreports  defect  1  ADR 0028, found 2026-09-21 while pricing a hidden-region narrowing
    # THE NEW CHECKER ADMITS A LOAN HELD ACROSS A MUTATION when the loan reaches
    # the result through a FIELD of a shared-reference parameter and TWO OR MORE
    # arguments carry a lifetime. The counter-based checker refuses it.
    #   #[borrow_carrying] struct B { p: &i64 }
    #   fn thru2(b: &B, d: &B) -> B { return B { p: b.p }; }
    #   let b0 = c.mk(); let bx = c.mk(); let b1 = thru2(&b0, &bx);
    #   c.bump(); return *b1.p;            // admitted by BIR, refused by the old
    # ROOT, MEASURED: `thru2` summarises `to_result=0` with `available=1`, so
    # relate_call's `narrow` block (which trusts that bit whenever >=2 args carry
    # lifetimes) drops every source and the result ties to nothing. The summary's
    # `taint_of` FieldRead arm short-circuits on `stored_shared_extract` (#77 r2
    # SEED) — the right answer to the CHECKER's question ("where does a new loan
    # root?") and the wrong answer to the SUMMARY's ("does a loan rooted at
    # parameter i reach the result?"). One predicate, two notions.
    # WHAT IT NEEDS TO BECOME A ROW: the repair is in borrow_flow_summary.inc,
    # which the OLD checker also consumes, so it moves verdicts on both sides and
    # must be priced with a whole-corpus run of its own before it is taken.
    # The one-argument spelling of the same shape is the twin defect in the other
    # direction: pass/bc_argcomp_tvbuild_byvalue_fat_admit is REFUSED by the new
    # checker because a hidden result region with a single candidate argument is
    # tied unconditionally. Both close together or neither does.

stream_plane_batch_region_unnamed  defect  2  ADR 0028 blocker, priced 2026-09-21
    # `SliceStream<R> { rows: &[R] }` hands out `RowsBatch<R>` (= `&[R]`, an ALIAS with
    # an ELIDED region) from `next_batch(&mut self)`. Under Rust's elision rule 3 the
    # batch's region is then `&mut self`'s, so the rows appear to borrow the STREAM
    # rather than the caller's slice, and anything built from a row and returned past
    # the stream's scope is refused. The ADR 0028 checker says so on three generated
    # WQL scans (wql_find_e2e: find_41_run, find_ancient_run, find_by_age_run):
    # "mutable borrow of '__ss0' (line N) is used after an access at line N invalidated
    # it". Same class as the str iterators fixed on 2026-09-21 and settled by Victor
    # that morning: a struct with a borrowed field NAMES its region.
    # PRICED WITH THE COMPILER, and this is why it is a row and not a commit: naming
    # the alias (`pub type RowsBatch<'a, R> = &'a [R];`) makes EVERY mention of it
    # require a lifetime argument, which forces `'a` into every enclosing signature and
    # type — `Buffer<R>`, `WritWalk`, `DeltaBatch<R>` and their users in turn. 37 alias
    # mentions and 64 `SliceStream` mentions across stdlib/lang/stream, stdlib/mem/stream,
    # stdlib/mem/wql/{writ_graph,params,rexpr_walk}. A first attempt was made and
    # REVERTED at the first build: the shape is mechanical but it cascades, and a
    # half-threaded region is worse than none.
    # WHAT IT NEEDS TO BECOME A ROUND: thread `'a` through the stream plane in one pass,
    # bottom-up (alias, SliceStream, Buffer, WritWalk, DeltaBatch, then the WQL
    # generator's emitted signatures), with `cmake --build` as the pricing instrument at
    # each step. It closes three ADR 0028 blockers and is the last known instance of the
    # elision class.

